    "updated_at": "2017-09-01T11:28:44Z",
    "body": "I have addressed the issues @bzbarsky and I noted earlier. I have also made the change to return `undefined` instead of `null` for `document.all()` and `document.all.item()`, as is the behavior in Chrome, Edge, and released Safari. The change to the latter isn't real clean because of the fact that IDL doesn't work well with `undefined`, but it's there for review.\r\n\r\nHowever, I also found even more complications to Web compatibility regarding this excrescence.\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nIt seems at least Chrome [supports](https://cs.chromium.org/chromium/src/third_party/WebKit/Source/bindings/core/v8/custom/V8HTMLAllCollectionCustom.cpp?l=136&rcl=0689c1f2a2a08982c6015f94671b3ff38485dbf7) a weird two-argument form of `document.all()` legacy caller: \r\n\r\n```c++\r\n  // If there is a second argument it is the index of the item we want.\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe good news is that this feature is used very very rarely (0.000002% according to Chrome's [UseCounter](https://www.chromestatus.com/metrics/feature/popularity#DocumentAllLegacyCallTwoArguments)), and this special treatment does not extend to `document.all.item()`. I will just ignore this new development.\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nAnother thing I noticed is that relatively recently WebKit [changed](https://cia.webkit.org/changeset/216851/webkit) its behavior regarding HTMLAllCollection to be spec-compliant, and therefore not consistent with [any other browsers except for Firefox](https://github.com/TimothyGu/document-all-undefined/blob/master/index.html) (mostly). :confused: \r\n\r\nCC'ing @samweinig who made that change.",
